{
  "dom_id": "step-q352390",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{5}+1,\\,y=\\frac{35}{6}{t}^{6}+2{t}^{5}+9.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=7t+2,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=7t+2,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{5}+1)=5{t}^{4}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(7t+2)}{5{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(7)}{5{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5{t}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\\frac{7}{5({2}^{4})}=\\frac{7}{80}.\n$$",
